{"ex-3A03-1":{"number":"1","title":"Image formed by a convex lens (I)","question":"<p>An object <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>A</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>B</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> is in front of a convex lens with foci <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>F</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>F</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>′. Light rays <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>p</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>q</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>r</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> coming from point <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>A</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> falls on the lens as shown.</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/b3a01a0fd6a3f061d616642c.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:316.7px\"/></p><p>(a) Locate the image <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>A</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>′<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>B</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>′ by completing rays <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>p</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>r</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>. What is the image distance?</p><p>(b) Hence, complete ray <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>q</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>.</p>","answer":"<p>(a)</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/7667e10399f6377e2d56033c.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:316.7px\"/></p><p>The image distance is 15 cm.</p><p>(b)</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/2a524d7f137b9e6c66af8ed1.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:316.7px\"/></p>","tips":"<p><b>Tip</b></p><p>Ray q is not included in the construction rules. However, since it comes from A, it must pass through A′. Similarly, a ray from B must pass through B′.</p>","think":"<p>The convex lens is replaced by one with a focal length of 15 cm. Suppose positions of the object and the lens are unchanged. Locate the new image of the object.</p>","thinkAnswer":"<p>The diagram gives an object distance <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>u</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>=</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>3</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>cm</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> and object height 10 cm. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>f</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>=</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>5</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>cm</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>, the object is now at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>f</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>. By <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>f</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mrow><mtext>=</mtext></mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>u</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mrow><mtext>+</mtext></mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>v</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>v</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mrow><mtext>=</mtext></mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>5</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mrow><mtext>−</mtext></mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>3</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>, giving <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>v</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>=</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>3</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>cm</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>.</p><p>The new image is real, inverted and the same size as the object. Place B′ on the principal axis 30 cm to the right of the lens and A′ 10 cm below B′. A ray from A parallel to the axis refracts through the new right-hand focus; a ray through the optical centre continues straight. Their intersection locates A′.</p>","location":"Book 3A  |  Chapter 3A03  |  Source PDF p. 14","source":{"name":"3A03 Examples.docx","sha256":"4867cf65811d93f9b2b062cb1b69f8218b2b32c473e34b8f1314350e78f37a91","block":3},"id":"ex-3A03-1","chapter":"3A03"},"ex-3A03-2":{"number":"2","title":"Image formed by a convex lens (II)","question":"<p>An object <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>A</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>B</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> is in front of a convex lens with foci <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>F</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>F</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>′. Light rays <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>p</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>q</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>,</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>r</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>s</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> coming from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>A</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>B</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> falls on the lens as shown. Sketch the image and the emerging rays.</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/c4035a9c5a7c075544b86d6e.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:440.1px\"/></p>","answer":"<p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/13238441808ac61a5aff1319.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:510.1px\"/></p>","tips":"<p><b>Tip</b></p><p>① Complete rays p and r according to the construction rules. ② Locate the image A′B′ by extending the rays in ① backwards. ③ Since ray q comes from A, it appears to come from A&#x27; after passing through the lens. ④ Since ray s comes from B, it appears to come from B′ after passing through the lens.</p>","think":"","thinkAnswer":"","location":"Book 3A  |  Chapter 3A03  |  Source PDF p. 15","source":{"name":"3A03 Examples.docx","sha256":"4867cf65811d93f9b2b062cb1b69f8218b2b32c473e34b8f1314350e78f37a91","block":23},"id":"ex-3A03-2","chapter":"3A03"},"ex-3A03-3":{"number":"3","title":"Parallel rays through a convex lens","question":"<p>Parallel rays <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>p</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>q</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> hit a convex lens as shown. Sketch the emerging rays.</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/80069c03ae411069005e78d7.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:256.1px\"/></p>","answer":"<p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/80bbfdda1ad3f8bf24a03c53.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:254.5px\"/></p>","tips":"<p><b>Tip</b></p><p>① According to rule 3, q travels straight on. ② Draw the focal plane. ③ Since p and q are parallel before passing through the lens, they must intersect on the focal plane.</p>","think":"","thinkAnswer":"","location":"Book 3A  |  Chapter 3A03  |  Source PDF p. 17","source":{"name":"3A03 Examples.docx","sha256":"4867cf65811d93f9b2b062cb1b69f8218b2b32c473e34b8f1314350e78f37a91","block":32},"id":"ex-3A03-3","chapter":"3A03"},"ex-3A03-4":{"number":"4","title":"Magnification of an image formed by a convex lens","question":"<p>An object of height 5 cm is placed at 15 cm from a convex lens of focal length 10 cm.</p><p>(a) Find the position and magnification of the image.</p><p>(b) How do the magnification change when the object is moved away from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>F</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>′?</p>","answer":"<p>(a)</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/65687872228de565a0b952ef.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:341.9px\"/></p><p>Image distance <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>v</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>=</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>3</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>cm</mtext></mrow></mrow></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>m</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>=</mtext></mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>v</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>u</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mrow><mtext>=</mtext></mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>3</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>5</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mrow><mtext>=</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></math></p><p>(b)</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/34b88103dd21a44b2cfc15e1.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:343.5px\"/></p><p>The magnification decreases.</p>","tips":"","think":"","thinkAnswer":"","location":"Book 3A  |  Chapter 3A03  |  Source PDF p. 21","source":{"name":"3A03 Examples.docx","sha256":"4867cf65811d93f9b2b062cb1b69f8218b2b32c473e34b8f1314350e78f37a91","block":41},"id":"ex-3A03-4","chapter":"3A03"},"ex-3A03-5":{"number":"5","title":"Image formed by a concave lens","question":"<p>An object <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>A</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>B</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> is placed in front of a concave lens. Light rays <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>p</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>q</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>r</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>s</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> coming from the object falls on the lens as shown.</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/5720c5b696053242e7a5e5ac.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:317.8px\"/></p><p>(a) Locate the image <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>A</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>′<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>B</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>′ by completing rays <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>q</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>r</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>.</p><p>(b) Hence, complete rays <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>p</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>s</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>.</p>","answer":"<p>(a)</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/d0bb9294bb844ed7919ca174.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:323.7px\"/></p><p>(b)</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/49dc9f27a9134b264ad86e79.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:506.6px\"/></p>","tips":"","think":"<p>How will the image distance change if the lens is replaced by another one with a longer focal length?</p>","thinkAnswer":"<p>For a concave lens, a longer focal length means a larger magnitude <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>F</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>=</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>|</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>f</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>|</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>. With object distance <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>u</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> fixed, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>−</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>F</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mrow><mtext>=</mtext></mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>u</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mrow><mtext>+</mtext></mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>v</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>v</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>=</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>−</mtext></mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>u</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>F</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>u</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>+</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>F</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>. Thus the magnitude of the image distance increases: the virtual image moves farther from the lens on the object side, towards the object. The signed image distance becomes more negative.</p>","location":"Book 3A  |  Chapter 3A03  |  Source PDF p. 26","source":{"name":"3A03 Examples.docx","sha256":"4867cf65811d93f9b2b062cb1b69f8218b2b32c473e34b8f1314350e78f37a91","block":54},"id":"ex-3A03-5","chapter":"3A03"},"ex-3A03-6":{"number":"6","title":"Parallel rays through a concave lens","question":"<p>Parallel rays <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>p</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>q</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> hit a concave lens as shown. Sketch the emerging rays.</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/b7acfdaedc74f4496d93ebed.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:259.1px\"/></p>","answer":"<p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/30b80170e54eb9ce5e0ad7d7.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:259.1px\"/></p>","tips":"<p><b>Tip</b></p><p>① According to rule 3, q travels straight on. ② Draw the focal plane. ③ Since p and q are parallel before passing through the lens, they appear to come from the same point on the focal plane after passing through the lens.</p>","think":"","thinkAnswer":"","location":"Book 3A  |  Chapter 3A03  |  Source PDF p. 28","source":{"name":"3A03 Examples.docx","sha256":"4867cf65811d93f9b2b062cb1b69f8218b2b32c473e34b8f1314350e78f37a91","block":69},"id":"ex-3A03-6","chapter":"3A03"},"ex-3A03-7":{"number":"7","title":"Magnification of an image formed by a concave lens","question":"<p>The figure shows the view through a concave lens of focal length 2 m. The lens is</p><p>2 m from the chair.</p><p>(a) State the nature of the image.</p><p>(b) Draw a ray diagram to locate the image of the</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/3d4f5a3fcb87824b0f19effe.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:315.1px\"/></p><p>chair.</p><p>(c) Find the magnification of the image.</p><p>(d) If the lens is moved closer to the chair, how will the magnification of the image change?</p>","answer":"<p>(a) The image is virtual, erect and diminished.</p><p>(b)</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/15c1b664f9aa4df3d558a8bc.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:270.0px\"/></p><p>(c) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>m</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>v</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>u</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = 0.5</p><p>(d)</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/85fa182a13452a04c90e8bad.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:272.2px\"/></p><p>The image will be larger, so the magnification will increase.</p>","tips":"","think":"","thinkAnswer":"","location":"Book 3A  |  Chapter 3A03  |  Source PDF p. 31","source":{"name":"3A03 Examples.docx","sha256":"4867cf65811d93f9b2b062cb1b69f8218b2b32c473e34b8f1314350e78f37a91","block":78},"id":"ex-3A03-7","chapter":"3A03"},"ex-3A03-8":{"number":"8","title":"Locating foci and images from one light ray","question":"<p>In Figure a, a light ray <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>s</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> is refracted by a lens <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>L</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> of focal length <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>f</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>X</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> is a point object lying on the principal axis of the lens.</p><p>(a) What type of lens is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>L</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>? Explain.</p><p>(b) Find <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>f</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>.</p><p>(c) Where is the image of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>X</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>?</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/48dcf277e71107c39cb8cd36.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:270.6px\"/></p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/20067bf3f1f6242554f7ddd4.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:41.4px\"/></p>","answer":"<p>(a) Since ray <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>s</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> is refracted away from the principle axis, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>L</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> is a concave lens.</p><p>(b) As shown in Figure b, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>f</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = 40 cm</p><p>(c) Suppose an object <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>A</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>B</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> is placed at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>X</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> (Fig c).</p><p>∴ The image of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>X</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> is 24 cm from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>L</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> (at the position of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>B</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>’</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>).</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/5372e0b78f32f1a62a358434.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:299.0px\"/></p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/853819435fe49ea734e24059.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:302.1px\"/></p>","tips":"","think":"","thinkAnswer":"","location":"Book 3A  |  Chapter 3A03  |  Source PDF p. 33","source":{"name":"3A03 Examples.docx","sha256":"4867cf65811d93f9b2b062cb1b69f8218b2b32c473e34b8f1314350e78f37a91","block":96},"id":"ex-3A03-8","chapter":"3A03"},"ex-3A03-9":{"number":"9","title":"Wide-angle adapter","question":"<p>A wide-angle adapter of a smartphone is shown in Figure a. Mary studies the adapter and finds that it consists of a lens <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>L</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>. Then she takes photos of the surrounding buildings with (Fig b) and without the adapter (Fig c).</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/f254fd0a99876d26b42e1c69.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:540.6px\"/></p><p>(a) Which type of lens is used in the adapter? Explain your answer.</p><p>(b) Mary looks through the adapter directly. In Figure d, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>A</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>B</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> represents an object in front of the lens <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>L</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> in the adaptor. Light ray <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>p</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> comes from point <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>A</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> and enter Mary’s eye after passing through <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>L</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>.</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/a103d794f612512057d54eb5.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:411.4px\"/></p><p>(i) Locate the image of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>A</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>B</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>. Label it <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>A</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>′<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>B</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>′.</p><p>(ii) Locate the foci of the lens.</p>","answer":"<p>(a) Since the image is erect and diminished, the lens is a concave lens.</p><p>(b) (i), (ii)</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/077f3158e1f2a19312eafbfc.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:362.2px\"/></p>","tips":"<p><b>Tip</b></p><p>① Draw a ray from A using rule 3. ② Extended ray p backwards. ③ The intersection is A′. Then draw A′B′ using a dotted arrow. ④ To locate F′, draw a ray from A using rule 1. The emerging ray appears to come from A′ and cut the principal axis at F′.</p>","think":"","thinkAnswer":"","location":"Book 3A  |  Chapter 3A03  |  Source PDF p. 34","source":{"name":"3A03 Examples.docx","sha256":"4867cf65811d93f9b2b062cb1b69f8218b2b32c473e34b8f1314350e78f37a91","block":111},"id":"ex-3A03-9","chapter":"3A03"},"ex-3A03-10":{"number":"10","title":"Lens formula","question":"<p>An object is 15 cm from a convex lens of focal length 10 cm.</p><p>(a) Find the image distance. Is the image real or virtual?</p><p>(b) If the convex lens is replaced by a concave lens of the same focal length, find the image distance. Is the image real or virtual?</p>","answer":"<p>(a) By <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>u</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> + <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>v</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>f</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>,</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>5</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> + <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>v</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>v</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = 30 cm</p><p>The image is real since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>v</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> is positive.</p><p>(b) By <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>u</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> + <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>v</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>f</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>,</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>5</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> + <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>v</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>–</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>v</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = –6 cm</p><p>The image is virtual since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>v</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> is negative.</p>","tips":"<p><b>Tip</b></p><p>f is negative for a concave lens.</p>","think":"","thinkAnswer":"","location":"Book 3A  |  Chapter 3A03  |  Source PDF p. 37","source":{"name":"3A03 Examples.docx","sha256":"4867cf65811d93f9b2b062cb1b69f8218b2b32c473e34b8f1314350e78f37a91","block":127},"id":"ex-3A03-10","chapter":"3A03"},"ex-3A03-12":{"number":"12","title":"Graph of 1v against 1u","question":"<p>Sam does Experiment 3e and obtains the graph on the right.</p><p>(a) Find the focal length of the lens.</p><p>(b) Sketch the graph obtained if he repeats the experiment with a lens of a shorter focal length.</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/a94991a06b32b275563851f5.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:227.0px\"/></p>","answer":"<p>(a) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>f</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = 0.04</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>f</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = 25 cm</p><p>(b)</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/89b689a50ec3841bb88219ab.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:219.8px\"/></p><p>Exercise 3.4 Q2 (p.118)</p>","tips":"<p><b>Skill</b></p><p><b>Mathematics</b></p><p>Linear relationship p.137</p>","think":"","thinkAnswer":"","location":"Book 3A  |  Chapter 3A03  |  Source PDF p. 39","source":{"name":"3A03 Examples.docx","sha256":"4867cf65811d93f9b2b062cb1b69f8218b2b32c473e34b8f1314350e78f37a91","block":144},"id":"ex-3A03-12","chapter":"3A03"}}